      "id": "cs-algorithms-1",
      "title": "Binary Search Complexity",
      "subject": "Computer Science",
      "topic": "Algorithms",
      "perseusFormat": {
        "question": {
          "content": "What is the time complexity of binary search on a sorted array of $n$ elements?\n\n[[☃ radio 1]]\n\n```python\ndef binary_search(arr, target):\n    left, right = 0, len(arr) - 1\n    while left <= right:\n        mid = (left + right) // 2\n        if arr[mid] == target:\n            return mid\n        elif arr[mid] < target:\n            left = mid + 1\n        else:\n            right = mid - 1\n    return -1\n```",
          "widgets": {
            "radio 1": {
              "type": "radio",
              "graded": true,
              "options": {
                "choices": [
                  { "content": "$O(\\log n)$", "correct": true },
                  { "content": "$O(n)$", "correct": false },
                  { "content": "$O(n^2)$", "correct": false },
                  { "content": "$O(1)$", "correct": false }
                ],
                "randomize": true
              },
              "version": { "major": 0, "minor": 0 }
            }
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        "hints": [
          {
            "content": "Binary search works by repeatedly dividing the search space in half.\n\nWith each comparison, we eliminate half of the remaining elements.",
            "widgets": {},
            "images": {}
          },
          {
            "content": "If we start with $n$ elements:\n- After 1 step: $n/2$ elements\n- After 2 steps: $n/4$ elements\n- After $k$ steps: $n/2^k$ elements\n\nWe stop when $n/2^k = 1$, so $k = \\log_2 n$",
            "widgets": {},
            "images": {}
          }
        ],
        "answerArea": { "type": "single" }
      }
